From 77c580ac405d9d6a6ac777eb201e98a7e87ff8af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Delgado <admin@sytue.eu>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 22:59:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] cache, container, painter, test: stop keeping everything
 ever themed alive

Three leaks, measured on the Sytue test suite (apps/mail, 78 screens): every
screen stayed in memory until the process ended, 1 GB after one run and
+~180 MB per -count; with this change the heap after the run is 145 MB, 44 MB
once the renderer cache expires, and it no longer grows with -count.

- ThemeOverride: the override scope kept against every covered object holds
  the theme, and the theme (featureTheme) pointed back at the container, which
  holds those same objects. It now points at a small themeFeatures struct.
- internal/cache: the override scopes were in a map keyed by the objects
  themselves. Only widgets were ever deleted (when their renderer expired), so
  canvas primitives under an override lived for good. With Go 1.24 the key is
  a weak pointer; older toolchains keep the previous map.
- internal/painter: the font cache was keyed by override scope, so every new
  ThemeOverride parsed the theme, emoji and symbol fonts again and kept them
  (55 % of that heap). It is keyed by the font the widget's theme gives.
- test: the queue of fyne.Do calls cleared each slot as it ran; reslicing kept
  the finished calls reachable from the backing array.

TestThemeOverride_DoesNotRetainContent and
TestCachedFontFace_SharedAcrossOverrideScopes fail with each change reverted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
